{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"thrilling-coplanar-hen","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Experiment: harness-parity oracle for completion verification (PoC, throwaway)\nOperator hypothesis: completion verification is conceptually simple and works exceptionally well as a plain subagent in an agent harness with free tools; the unreliability (false rejections, insufficiency loops) lives in OUR in-process scaffolding (step budget, structured tool surface, mid-loop output contract), not in the task. This PoC measures that directly: same corpus, production verifier vs a minimal harness agent.\n\n## Arms\n- **Arm A (control): production in-process verifier** — `verify-completion` runs on the corpus tickets with the SAME harness seams the filter experiment (fabulous-lowborn-guanaco) proved: isolated per-run verdict-cache namespaces (monkeypatch `cache_dir`, completion_verdict_cache.py:59), `source='local'`/signable=false, run from a throwaway worktree's git-ignored `.expscratch/`. WHERE the guanaco control arm's transcripts are reusable (same ticket, same tree/ref, per-AC verdicts recorded), REUSE them as Arm A data instead of paying for new runs; rerun only for pairing gaps.\n- **Arm B (treatment): minimal harness agent** — for each corpus ticket, a FRESH local harness subagent (Copilot CLI task agent; free, non-Bedrock) receives ONLY: the ticket's AC list verbatim, the worktree path at the pinned ref, and the instruction to verify each AC and emit JSON `{ac, verdict: PASS|FAIL, citations[]}` with file:line evidence. No rebar tooling, no step budget, no structured tool surface — plain grep/read/test-run freedom. One subagent per ticket per rep (not per AC — per-criterion fan-out was already implemented, measured, and removed for exploding cost per ticket 12b7's FORCE_CLOSE record; this experiment must not resurrect it).\n\n## Corpus\nReuse the guanaco corpus verbatim for cross-experiment comparability: 45c1 (bettong, task, 6 ACs), 3388 (shrew, story, 10 ACs), 0e68 (wildebeast, epic, 7 ACs + 14 children), each with its in-harness adversarial variant (5 planted-unmet ACs citing symbols proven absent at ref). Ground truth: real variants = all-PASS; adversarial planted ACs = FAIL. Known Arm-A noise: guanaco observed 2 agentic false-FAILs in one epic run and 35↔55 step variance.\n\n## Metrics (per arm, per ticket, per rep)\nFalse-PASS count (critical, per planted AC); false-FAIL count (the pain point, per real AC); per-AC agreement with ground truth; wall time; Arm A Bedrock spend (tokens + $); Arm B local-harness turn/tool-call counts (cost qualitative — local harness is free per operator).\n\n## Budget & reps\nBedrock cap **$20 total** (operator-approved) for Arm A reruns — track cumulative spend per run and STOP dispatching Arm A runs at the cap (reuse + partial pairing is an acceptable degraded result; report what was covered). Arm B is uncapped in dollars (local harness free) but capped at 2 reps/ticket-variant. Target: 2 reps per ticket-variant per arm; degrade to 1 rep on the epic if spend requires.\n\n## Predeclared decision rule\n**GO** (pursue an external-harness verifier seam in production design, feeding viral-peppy-lowchen) iff, aggregated over the corpus: (i) Arm B false-PASS = 0, AND (ii) Arm B total false-FAILs strictly < Arm A total false-FAILs, AND (iii) Arm B median wall time ≤ 2× Arm A. Anything else = NO-GO or INDETERMINATE (state which and why). Apply verbatim; no post-hoc rule edits.\n\n## Non-goals\nProduction integration (design goes to lowchen); per-AC fan-out (12b7); prompt engineering beyond the fixed minimal Arm-B instruction (one prompt, frozen before the first run, included in the report); closing this ticket (left in_progress for operator review).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Arm B prompt frozen and recorded on this ticket BEFORE the first Arm B run.\n- [ ] All 6 corpus ticket-variants exercised in both arms (reuse counted for Arm A), ≥1 rep each, with per-AC verdict tables.\n- [ ] Metrics table posted as a comment on this ticket AND on viral-peppy-lowchen: false-PASS/false-FAIL per arm, agreement, wall time, Arm A spend vs the $20 cap.\n- [ ] Predeclared GO/NO-GO rule applied verbatim with the arithmetic shown.\n- [ ] Zero Gerrit pushes; all runs source=local; harness confined to a git-ignored `.expscratch/`; Bedrock spend ≤ $20 documented.","id":"8554-1b0b-c3d7-4993","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Experiment: harness-parity oracle — production verifier vs minimal free-tool harness agent (PoC)"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786809665895127001,"uuid":"4e07926f-4940-425d-8e21-85d3004d88c4"}